You will see several distinct templating patterns:

A. Dot Notation and Scoping (.Values)
The leading dot (.) represents the root context scope.

.Values accesses anything declared in values.yaml.

.Release contains metadata about the release itself (e.g., {{ .Release.Name }} evaluates to dev-web, 
and {{ .Release.Namespace }} evaluates to default).

.Chart accesses fields from Chart.yaml (e.g., {{ .Chart.Version }}).

B. Template Functions & Pipelines (|)
Helm inherits Go template functions and includes the Sprig template library. Notice how values are 
piped into functions:

imagePullPolicy: {{ .Values.image.pullPolicy }}
image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}"

Pipelines (|): Take the output of the left side and pass it as an argument to the right side (identical to Linux pipes).

default: If .Values.image.tag is empty or omitted, it falls back to .Chart.AppVersion.

quote: Wraps the resulting string in double quotes ({{ .Values.myString | quote }}).

C. Indentation Control (indent and nindent)
YAML is strictly whitespace-sensitive. Helm solves indentation issues using nindent:

labels:
  {{- include "web-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}

  nindent 4 inserts a newline first, then indents the included snippet by 4 spaces.

The hyphen {{- strips leading whitespace from the template tag itself, preventing accidental blank lines.


